 Smart Brevity™ count: 1,482 words ... 5½ mins. Thanks to Noah Bressner for orchestrating. Edited by Bill Kole. ⚖️ Nine family members of the Kennedy political dynasty asked a judge to block President Trump's planned memorial arch, saying the 250-foot structure would cast a shadow over their relatives' gravesites at Arlington National Cemetery. Read the filing ... Get the latest . 

 🪖 House Republicans voted to block an effort to impeach Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth last night, shielding him from a politically embarrassing rebuke. A 211-204 procedural vote killed the resolution. Go deeper . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing — Miller&#x27;s focus: MAGA and men 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Stephen Miller and Natalie Harp walk to Marine One on Friday. Photo: Saul Loeb/AFP via Getty Images Stephen Miller wants to use his new role as White House chief of staff to win back young and minority men who helped elect President Trump in 2024 but swung toward Democrats in the midterms, two people familiar with the administration's plans tell Axios' Alex Isenstadt. 

 Why it matters: Miller, named last week to replace Susie Wiles, plans to focus on reviving the populist agenda that helped the president build his 2024 coalition, with an emphasis on working-class voters. Young, working-class and minority men fled the GOP in droves in last week's elections , amid high prices fueled by Trump's tariffs, the war in Iran, and concerns about AI's impact on the job market.

 Trump won men under 30 by 14 points in 2024, according to a Tufts study . Last week, Democrats won them by 22 points in House races — a 36-point shift. Latino men also swung sharply toward Democrats, as the administration's hard-line immigration crackdown became an increasingly contentious political issue. 🔎 Zoom in: Miller wants to spend more time highlighting administration policies he believes haven't gotten enough attention, and that will appeal to working-class voters concerned about the economy, insiders tell Axios.

 His priorities will sound familiar to Trump watchers: eliminating what the administration considers "woke" policies from colleges and workplaces, lowering prescription drug prices, and rooting out government and health-care fraud. He also wants to emphasize reducing the federal bureaucracy and push for permitting reform , while highlighting how those efforts could help address affordability. Sources in the business community say Miller's ideological drive makes him mostly impervious to typical Washington lobbying. 👓 Between the lines: With Democrats taking control of Congress in January, Miller will have to advance that agenda without the help of Republican majorities in either the House or Senate.

 Some of his plans could be pursued through executive orders by Trump. Others, including permitting reform, would require bipartisan cooperation. Reality check: House Minority Leader Hakeem Jeffries (D-N.Y.), who's in line to become House speaker in January, has said he has "zero interest" in working with Miller, whose immigration policies have made him a frequent target of Democratic attacks.

