 3. 🥊 House GOP bets on MAGA &quot;fighter&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Rep. Jim Jordan (R-Ohio), with outgoing Speaker Mike Johnson behind him, talks to reporters before a House GOP meeting in the Capitol yesterday. Photo: Kenny Holston/The New York Times House Republicans are turning to one of their fiercest fighters to lead their diminished conference after one of their biggest drubbings in decades, Axios' Kate Santaliz writes. 

 Why it matters: Jim Jordan, 62, a founding member of the hard-line House Freedom Caucus, has completed a stunning insurgent-to-establishment turn after spending much of his nearly 20 years in Congress battling Republican leaders from the right. Jordan's 106-73 win over House Majority Leader Steve Scalise (R-La.) is a bet by Republicans that they shouldn't move away from President Trump to claw their way back to power.

 "I think when you take a beating like we took, you need to make some changes, and I also think we're gearing up for war," Rep. Randy Fine (R-Fla.), who backed Jordan, told us. Share this story . 

 
 
